<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="s_det_II" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Further properties of the determinant</title>
<subsection xml:id="ss_det_multilinear_alt">
<title>Determinant is alternating and multilinear</title>
<p>
  For the next theorem, it is convenient to think of the determinant a function of the <m>n</m> rows <m>\boldr_1,\boldr_2,\dots, \boldr_n</m> of an <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>. That is, instead of thinking of <m>\det</m> as a function <m>\det\colon \ML_{n,n}(F)\rightarrow F</m>, setting <m>V=F^n</m>, we will think of it as a function 
  <md>
    <mrow>\det\colon V^n \amp \rightarrow F </mrow>
    <mrow>(\boldr_1,\boldr_2,\dots, \boldr_n) \amp \mapsto \det(\boldr_1,\boldr_2,\dots, \boldr_n)</mrow>
  </md>.
  To facilitate arguments in this vein, we will adopt temporarily that convention of writing a matrix <m>A</m> whose <m>i</m>-th row is <m>\boldr_i</m> as 
  <md>
    A=(\boldr_1,\boldr_2,\dots, \boldr_n)
  </md>.
  Yes, we are taking some notational liberties here, but we will drop this convention after this section is complete!
</p>
<theorem xml:id="th_det_multilinear">
  <title>Determinant: alternating,skew-symmetric, multilinear</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>F</m> be a field, let <m>n</m> be a positive integer, and let <m>V=F^n</m>. We consider <m>\det</m> as a function on <m>V^n</m> by defining <m>\det(\boldr_1,\boldr_2,\dots, \boldr_n)</m> as <m>\det A</m>, where <m>A=(\boldr_1,\boldr_2,\dots, \boldr_n)</m>. 
      <ol>
        <li>
          <title>Alternating property</title>
          <p>
            If <m>(\boldr_1,\boldr_2,\dots, \boldr_n)\in V</m> satisfies <m>\boldr_i=\boldr_j</m> for some indices <m>i\ne j</m>, then <m>\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_n)=0</m>. 
            In terms of matrices, if a matrix <m>A</m> has two identical rows, then <m>\det A=0</m>. 
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <title>Multilinear property</title>
          <p>
            Given any <m>(r_i)_{i=1}^n\in V^n</m>, and index <m>1\leq k\leq n</m>, and any vector <m>\bolds_i\in F^n</m>, and scalar <m>c\in F</m>, we have 
          <md number="yes" >
              <mrow xml:id="eq_det_additive">\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_i+\bolds_i,\dots, \boldr_n)\amp =\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_n)+\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \bolds_i,\dots, \boldr_n)</mrow>
              <mrow xml:id="eq_det_scalar">\det(\boldr_1,\dots, c\boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_n)\amp =c\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_n)</mrow>
            </md>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Skew-symmetric</title>
          <p>
            Suppose <m>1\leq i&lt; j\leq n</m>. We have 
            <md number="yes" xml:id="eq_det_skew_symmetric">
              \det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_j,\dots, \boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_n)=-\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_j,\dots,\boldr_n)
            </md>.
            In terms of matrices, if <m>B</m> is the result of swapping two rows of a matrix <m>A</m>, then <m>\det B=-\det A</m>. 
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <proof>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
     The alternating property is true vacuously for <m>n=1</m>, and can be easily verified for <m>n=2</m> via a direct computation. An induction argument then easily proves the result for all <m>n\geq 2</m> by expanding along a row of the matrix <m>A</m> that is not equal to <m>\boldr_i</m> or <m>\boldr_j</m>. We leave this to the reader.  
    </p>
  </li>

    <li>
      <p>
      We can prove the multilinear properties of (2) directly by expanding along the <m>k</m>-th row of the corresponding matrix. In more detail, let <m>A=(\boldr_1,\boldr_2,\dots, \boldr_n)</m>, where <m>\boldr_i=(r_{i,1},\dots, r_{i,n})</m> for all <m>i</m>,  and let <m>B=(\boldr_1,\dots, \bolds_i, \dots, \boldr_n)</m>, where <m>\bolds_i=(s_{k,1},s_{k,2},\dots, s_{k,n})</m>. Letting <m>C=(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_i+\bolds_i,\dots, \boldr_n)</m>,  we have 
      <md>
        <mrow>\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_i+\bolds_i,\dots, \boldr_n) =\det C=</mrow>
        <mrow> \sum_{j=1}^n (-1)^{k+j}(r_{i,j}+s_{i,j})\det C_{i,j}=</mrow>
        <mrow> \sum_{j=1}^n (-1)^{k+j}r_{i,j}\det C_{i,j}+\sum_{j=1}^n (-1)^{k+j}s_{i,j}\det C_{i,j}=</mrow>
        <mrow> \sum_{j=1}^n (-1)^{k+j}r_{i,j}\det A_{i,j}+ \sum_{j=1}^n(-1)^{k+j}s_{i,j}\det B_{i,j}=</mrow>
        <mrow> \det A+\det B=</mrow>
        <mrow> \det(\boldr_1,\dots,\boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_n)+\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \bolds_i,\dots, \boldr_n) </mrow>
      </md>,
      where we have used the fact that <m>C_{i,j}=A_{i,j}=B_{i,j}</m> as these three matrices are identical except for their <m>k</m>-th rows. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Similarly, we have 
      <md>
        <mrow>\det (\boldr_1,\dots, c\boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_n) \amp = \sum_{j=1}^n(-1)^{k+j}cr_{i,j}\det A_{i,j}</mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = c\sum_{j=1}^n(-1)^{k+j}r_{i,j}\det A_{i,j} </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp = c\det(\boldr_1,\boldr_2,\dots, \boldr_n) </mrow>
      </md>.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      The skew-symmetric property now follows from the alternating and multilinear properties. Given <m>1\leq i&lt; j\leq n</m>, we have 
      <md>
        <mrow>0 =\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \underset{i-\text{th entry}}{\boldr_i+\boldr_j},\dots, \underset{j-\text{th entry}}{\boldr_i+\boldr_j},\dots, \boldr_n) </mrow>
      </md>,
      by the alternating property. Expanding out the right side of the equation above using the multilinear property in succession yields
      <md>
        <mrow>\det(\boldr_1,\dots,\boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_i,\dots,\boldr_n)+\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_j,\dots, \boldr_n)
           </mrow>
          <mrow>+\det(\boldr_1,\dots,\boldr_j,\dots, \boldr_j,\dots,\boldr_n)
            +\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_j,\dots, \boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_n) </mrow>
        <mrow>=\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_j,\dots, \boldr_n)+\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_j,\dots, \boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_n)</mrow>
      </md>,
      since 
      <md>
        \det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_i,\dots,\boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_n)=\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_j,\dots, \boldr_j,\dots, \boldr_n)=0
      </md>
      by the alternating property again. 
      In all, we have 
      <md>
        0=\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_j,\dots, \boldr_n)+\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_j,\dots, \boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_n)
      </md>,
      or equivalently, 
      <md>
        \det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_j,\dots, \boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_n)=-\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_i,\dots, \boldr_j,\dots, \boldr_n)
      </md>.
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>
</p>
  </proof>
</theorem>
<p>
 The two properties <xref first="eq_det_additive" last="eq_det_scalar"/> taken together are summarized by saying the determinant is <em>linear</em> in each row, and hence <em>multilinear</em> overall. Note that by using the two properties in succession, we have
<md number="yes" xml:id="eq_det_multilinear">
  \det(\boldr_1,\dots, c\boldr_i+d\bolds_i,\dots, \boldr_n)=c\det(\boldr_1,\dots,\boldr_i,\dots \boldr_n)+d\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \bolds_i,\dots, \boldr_n)
 </md>
 for all <m>c,d\in F</m>. But what do these properties mean about the determinant of matrices? 
 Property <xref ref="eq_det_scalar"/> is easy enough to express in terms of matrices: if you scale a single row of a matrix <m>A</m> by <m>c</m>, then the value of the determinant is scaled by <m>c</m>. Property <xref ref="eq_det_additive"/>, on the other hand, is a little more convoluted. Its treatment in the proof of <xref ref="th_det_multilinear"/> probably is the best articulation of this: if 
 <md>
  <mrow>A\amp =(\boldr_1,\dots,\boldr_i,\dots \boldr_n)</mrow>
  <mrow>B\amp =(\boldr_1,\dots, \bolds_i,\dots, \boldr_n)</mrow>
  <mrow>C\amp =(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_i+\bolds_i,\dots, \boldr_n)</mrow>
 </md>, then 
 <md>
  \det C=\det A+\det B
 </md>.
 That ends up being a very powerful property of the determinant. In particular, when we pick <m>\bolds_i</m> to be <m>c\boldr_j</m>, for some other index <m>i</m>, we conclude that 
 <md>
  <mrow>\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_i+c\boldr_j) \amp = \det(\boldr_1,\dots,\boldr_i, \boldr_n)+c\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_j,\dots, \boldr_n) </mrow>
  <mrow> \amp=\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_n)+0 </mrow>
  <mrow> \amp =\det(\boldr_1,\dots, \boldr_n)</mrow>
 </md>,
 where the second to last equality follows from the fact that the matrix obtained by replacing the <m>i</m>-th row with the <m>j</m>-th row has two identical rows, and hence determinant equal to zero. We have just shown that the row addition operation leaves the determinant of a matrix unchanged! Add to this the skew-symmetric property of the determinant, and our comment above about scaling single rows, and we have the following complete description of the effect of row operations on the determinant. For simplicity, we express the result in terms of elementary matrices multiplied on the left: recall that this is the same thing as applying an elementary row operation. 
</p>
  <theorem xml:id="th_det_row_ops">
    <title>Row operations and determinant</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Let <m>A\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m>. 
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>For all <m>1\leq i\leq n</m> and <m>c\in F</m>, we have  
            <md>\det(\underset{c\cdot r_i}{E}\, A)=c\det(A)</md>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p> For all <m>1\leq i&lt; j\leq n</m>, we have
            <md>\det(\underset{r_i\leftrightarrow r_j}{E}\, A)=-\det(A)</md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            For all <m>1\leq i,j\leq n</m> and <m>c\in F</m>, we have
            <md>\det(\underset{r_i+cr_j}{E}\, A)=\det(A)</md>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      In particular, taking <m>A=I</m> above, we derive the following determinant formulas for elementary matrices: 
      <md number="yes">
        <mrow>\det \underset{c\, r_i}{E} \amp =c
         \amp  \det\underset{r_i\leftrightarrow r_j}{E}\amp=-1 
          \amp \det\underset{r_i+cr_j}{E}\amp=1
        </mrow>
      </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
    <remark xml:id="rm_det_row_ops">
  <p>
    In the language of row operations, <xref ref="th_det_row_ops"/> translates as follows:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Scaling a row of a matrix by <m>c</m> has the effect of scaling the determinant by <m>c</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Swapping two rows of a matrix changes the sign of the determinant.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Performing a row addition operation on a matrix has no effect on the determinant.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</remark>
    <!-- <remark xml:id="rm_det_col_ops">
      <title>Column operations and the determinant</title>
  <p>
  As shown in <xref ref="ex_det_col_ops"/> the determinant behaves in a similar manner with respect to <em>elementary column operations</em>: <ie />, scaling a column by a nonzero constant scales the determinant by <m>c</m>, swapping columns multiplies the determinant by <m>-1</m>, adding a multiple of one column to another leaves the determinant unchanged.
  </p>
</remark> -->

<corollary xml:id="cor_det_prod_elem">
  <title>Determinant and products of elementary matrices</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>A\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m>, and suppose we have
      <md number="yes" xml:id="eq_row_reduce_elem">
        B=E_rE_{r-1}\cdots E_1\, A
      </md>
      for some collection of elementary matrices <m>E_i</m>.  Then
      <md number="yes" xml:id="eq_det_row_reduce">
        \det B=\det E_r\, \det E_{r-1}\cdots \det E_1\, \det A
      </md>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <proof>
    <p>
      This is an easy proof by induction on the number <m>r</m> of elementary matrices involved. The base case is actually covered by <xref ref="th_det_row_ops"/> (the properties (1)-(3) and the determinant formulas), since we have 
      <md>
        <mrow>\det \underset{c\, r_i}{E}A \amp =c\det A=\det \underset{c\, r_i}{E}\det A </mrow>
        <mrow>\det \underset{r_i\leftrightarrow r_j}{E}A \amp =-1\det A=\det \underset{r_i\leftrightarrow r_j}{E}\det A </mrow>
        <mrow>\det \underset{r_i+cr_j}{E}A \amp =\det A=\det \underset{r_i+cr_j}{E}\det A </mrow>
      </md>.
    </p>
  </proof>
</corollary>
<p>
  <xref ref="cor_det_prod_elem"/> has both computational and theoretical applications.
</p>
<p> On the computational side, it suggests an alternative method of computing <m>\det A</m>: first row reduce <m>A</m> to a simpler matrix <m>B</m>, making sure to keep track of the operations you use; set up an equation as in <xref ref="eq_row_reduce_elem"/> representing the row reduction; then solve the corresponding equation <xref ref="eq_det_row_reduce"/> for <m>\det A</m> in terms of <m>\det B</m> and the <m>\det E_i</m>.
</p>
<example>
  <title>Determinant via row reduction </title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Suppose the matrix <m>A</m> can be row reduced to
      <md> B=\begin{amatrix}[rrr]2\amp -7\amp -8\\ 0\amp -3\amp 4\\ 0\amp 0\amp 2  \end{amatrix}</md>
      by perfomring the following sequence of row operations:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            First swap the second and third rows.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Then scale the first row by <m>3</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Then replace the second row with the second row plus the first row.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      Compute <m>\det A</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      In terms of elementary matrices we have
      <md>
        B=\underset{r_2+r_1}{E}\underset{3r_1}{E}\underset{r_2\leftrightarrow r_3}{E}A,
      </md>
      and hence
      <md>
        \det B=\det \underset{r_2+r_1}{E}\, \det \underset{3r_1}{E}\, \det \underset{r_2\leftrightarrow r_3}{E}\, \det A=(1)(3)(-1)\det A=(-3)\det A.
      </md>
      We conclude that
      <md>
        \det A=-\frac{1}{3}\det B=-(1/3)(-12)=4
      </md>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>

<p>
  On the theoretical side, <xref ref="cor_det_prod_elem"/> implies both <xref ref="th_inv_iff_det"/> and <xref ref="th_det_mult"/>.
</p>
<theorem xml:id="th_inv_iff_det">
  <title>Determinant and invertibility</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
    Let <m>A\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m>. The following conditions are equivalent. 
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>A</m> is invertible.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>\det A\ne 0</m>.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <proof>
    <p>
     Given <m>A\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m> we can row reduce <m>A</m> to a matrix <m>U</m> in reduced row echelon form. Equivalently, we have 
     <md>
        E_rE_{r-1}\cdots E_1A=U
     </md>
     for some elementary matrices <m>E_i</m>. By <xref ref="cor_det_prod_elem"/>, we have
     <md>
        <mrow>\right(\prod_{i=1}^r\det E_i\left)\det A \amp =\det U </mrow>
     </md>.
     Since <m>\det E_i\ne 0</m> for all <m>1\leq i\leq r</m>, it follows that <m>\det A\ne 0</m> if and only if <m>\det U\ne 0</m>. Since <m>U</m> is in reduced row echelon form, it upper triangular, and hence <m>\det U\ne 0</m> if and only if <m>U</m> has a leading one in each column, if and only if <m>U=I_n</m>. Taken all together, we see that 
     <md>
        <mrow>A \text{ invertible} \amp \iff U=I_n</mrow>
        <mrow> \amp \iff \det U\ne 0</mrow>
        <mrow> \amp \iff \det A\ne 0</mrow>
     </md>.
    </p>
  </proof>
</theorem>
<p>
  Before moving on, let's add our new invertibility condition to our ever-growing invertibility theorem. The equivalence of <xref ref="inv2_statement_det">statement</xref> is a direct consequence of <xref ref="th_inv_iff_det"/>. The equivalence of <xref ref="inv2_statement_exists_sol">statement</xref> to invertibility was given earlier as an exercise.
</p>
<theorem xml:id="th_invertibility_expanded">
  <title>Invertibility theorem</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>A\in\ML_{n,n}(F)</m>. The following statements are equivalent.
      <ol>
        <li xml:id="inv2_statement_def">
          <p>
            <m>A</m> is invertible.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="inv2_statement_unique_sol">
          <p>
            The matrix equation <md>A\underset{n\times 1}{\boldx}=\underset{n\times 1}{\boldb}</md> has a unique solution for any <m>\boldb\in \R^n</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li xml:id="inv2_statement_exists_sol">
          <p>
            The matrix equation <md>A\underset{n\times 1}{\boldx}=\underset{n\times 1}{\boldb}</md> has a solution for any <m>\boldb\in \R^n</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li xml:id="inv2_statement_trivial_sol">
          <p>
            The matrix equation <md>A\underset{n\times 1}{\boldx}=\underset{n\times 1}{\boldzero}</md> has a unique solution: namely, <m>\boldx=\boldzero_{n\times 1}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li xml:id="inv2_statement_row_equiv">
          <p>
            <m>A</m> is row equivalent to <m>I_n</m>,
            the <m>n\times n</m> identity matrix.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li xml:id="inv2_statement_prod_elems">
          <p>
            <m>A</m> is a product of elementary matrices.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li xml:id="inv2_statement_det">
          <p>
            <m>\det A\ne 0</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>
<p>
    Next we come to an equally important property of the determinant: the <em>multiplicative property</em>. 
</p>
<theorem xml:id="th_det_mult">
  <title>Determinant is multiplicative</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Given matrices <m>A,B\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m>, we have 
      <md>
        \det AB=\det A\, \det B
      </md>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <proof>
    <p>
      We consider two cases based on the invertibility of <m>A</m> and/or <m>B</m>.
    </p>
    <case>
     <title><m>A</m> or <m>B</m> is not invertible</title>
     <p>In this case <m>AB</m> is not invertible (<xref ref="cor_inv_prod_eq"/>), and hence <m>\det AB=0</m> by <xref ref="th_inv_iff_det"/>. By the same reasoning we must have <m>\det A=0</m> or <m>\det B=0</m>. It follows that
     <md>
       \det AB=\det A\, \det B=0
     </md>
     in this case.
    </p>
    </case>
    <case>
     <title><m>A</m> and <m>B</m> both invertible</title>
    <p>
    In this case we can write
    <md>
      <mrow> A \amp E_1E_2\cdots E_r \amp B\amp=E'_1E'_2\cdots E'_s </mrow>
    </md>
    for some elementary matrices <m>E_i</m> and <m>E'_j</m> (<xref ref="th_invertibility"/>). Then
    <md>
      <mrow> \det AB \amp= \det(E_1E_2\cdots E_rE'_1E'_2\cdots E'_s) </mrow>
      <mrow> \amp= \det E_1\det E_2\cdots \det E_r\det E'_1\det E'_2\cdots \det E'_s \amp (<xref ref="cor_det_prod_elem"/>)</mrow>
      <mrow>  \amp=\det A\, \det B \amp (<xref ref="cor_det_prod_elem"/>) </mrow>
    </md>.
    </p>
    </case>
  </proof>
</theorem>
<p>
    Now that we know <m>\det</m> is a multiplicative map, we can easily derive a result describing how the determinant behaves with respect to <em>column operations</em>. Using the column method of multiplication, we see that an elementary matrix <m>E</m>, when multiplying a matrix <m>A</m> on the right, performs the corresponding column operation on <m>A</m>. In more detail: 
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>A\underset{c\, r_i}{E}</m> is the result of scaling the <m>i</m>-th column of <m>A</m> by <m>c</m>; 
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>A\underset{r_i\leftrightarrow r_j}{E}</m> is the result of swapping the <m>i</m>-th and <m>j</m>-th columns of <m>A</m>; 
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>A\underset{r_i+cr_j}{E}</m> is the result of replacing the <m>i</m>-th column of <m>A</m> with the <m>i</m>-th column plus <m>c</m> times the <m>j</m>-th column of <m>A</m>.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul> 
    In other words, scaling a column of <m>A</m> by <m>c</m> scales the determinant by <m>c</m>, swapping two columns changes the sign of the determinant, and adding a multiple of one column to another leaves the determinant unchanged.
</p>
<corollary xml:id="cor_det_column_ops">
    <title>Determinant and column operations</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
      Let <m>A\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m>. 
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>For all <m>1\leq i\leq n</m> and <m>c\in F</m>, we have  
            <md>\det(A\, \underset{c\cdot r_i}{E})=c\det(A)</md>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p> For all <m>1\leq i&lt; j\leq n</m>, we have
            <md>\det(A\, \underset{r_i\leftrightarrow r_j}{E})=-\det(A)</md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            For all <m>1\leq i,j\leq n</m> and <m>c\in F</m>, we have
            <md>\det(A\, \underset{r_i+cr_j}{E})=\det(A)</md>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
        </p>
    </statement>
</corollary>
<p>
    We will add one more theoretical result to this section that already bristles with theory! Let <m>V=\F^n</m>. It turns out that not only is <m>\det</m> an alternating multilinear map on <m>V^n</m>, it is (almost) the <em>only</em> such map! Let's make this more precise. 
</p>
<theorem xml:id="th_det_unique">
<title>Determinant: unique characterization</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>V=F^n</m> where <m>n</m> is a positive integer, and treat <m>\det</m> as a map on <m>V^n</m>.
<ol>
    <li>
        <p>
            <m>\det</m> is the unique alternating multilinear map on <m>V^n</m> that further satisfies
            <md>
                \det(\bolde_1,\bolde_2,\dots, \bolde_n)=\det I_n=1
            </md>.
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            If <m>f\colon V^n\rightarrow F</m> is an alternating multilinear map, then <m>f=c\det</m>, where 
            <md>
                f(\bolde_1,\bolde_2,\dots, \bolde_n)=f(I_n)=c
            </md>. 
        </p>
    </li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<proof>
<p>
    Left as a homework exercise. 
</p>
</proof>
</theorem>
<p>
    As evidence of how such a characterization can be useful, we can now give a proof that <m>\det A^T=\det A</m> that works for any field <m>F</m>. 
</p>
<corollary xml:id="cor_det_transpose">
    <statement>
        <p>
            For all <m>A\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m>, we have 
            <md>
                \det A^T=\det A
            </md>.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Left as homework exercise. 
        </p>
    </proof>
</corollary>

</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ss_adjoint_matrix">
<title>Adjoint matrix</title>
<definition xml:id="d_adjoint">
  <title>Adjoint matrix</title>
  <idx><h>adjoint matrix</h></idx>
  <notation>
    <usage><m>\adj A</m></usage>
    <description>adjoint of a square matrix</description>
  </notation>
  <statement>
    <p>
Let <m>A\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m>, and let <m>I=\{1,2\dots, n\}\times \{1,2,\dots, n\}</m>. The <term>adjoint matrix</term> of <m>A</m>, denoted <m>\adj A</m>, is the <m>n\times n</m> matrix defined as
    <md>
     \adj A=[(-1)^{i+j}M_{j,i}]_{(i,j)\in I}=\left( [(-1)^{i+j}M_{i,j}]_{(i,j)\in I}\right)^T
    </md>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>
    <remark xml:id="rm_adjoint">
  <p>
    Be careful of the order reversal in this definition. The <m>i,j</m>-th entry of <m>\adj A</m> is equal to plus or minus the <m>j,i</m>-th minor of <m>A</m>. Let's see this in action for some small matrices.
  </p>
  <p>
    For <md>A=\abcdmatrix{a}{b}{c}{d}</md> we have
    <md>
      \adj A=\begin{amatrix}[rr] M_{11}\amp -M_{21}\\ -M_{12}\amp M_{22}\end{amatrix}=
      \begin{amatrix}[rr]d\amp -b\\ -c\amp a  \end{amatrix}
    </md>.
  </p>
  <p>
    For
    <md>
      A=\begin{bmatrix}
        a\amp b\amp c\\ d\amp e\amp f\\ g\amp h\amp i
    \end{bmatrix}
    </md>
    we have
    <md>
      \adj A=\begin{amatrix}[rrr]
        M_{1,1}\amp -M_{2,1}\amp M_{3,1}\\
        -M_{1,2}\amp M_{2,2}\amp -M_{3,2}\\
        M_{1,3}\amp -M_{2,3}\amp M_{3,3}
    \end{amatrix}
    =\begin{amatrix}[rrr]
      (ei-fh)\amp -(bi-ch)\amp (bf-ce)\\
      -(di-fg)\amp (ai-cg)\amp -(af-cd)\\
      (dh-eg)\amp -(ah-bg)\amp (ae-bd)
  \end{amatrix}
    </md>.
  </p>
</remark>
<theorem xml:id="th_adjoint">
  <title>Adjoint matrix formula</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Given <m>A\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m>, we have
    <md number="yes" xml:id="eq_adj_form">
      A\,(\adj A)=(\adj A)\, A=(\det A)I
    </md>.
      As a consequence <m>A</m> is invertible if and only if <m>\det A\ne 0</m>, and in this case we have 
      <md number="yes" xml:id="eq_adj_inv_form">
        A^{-1}=\frac{1}{\det A}\, \adj A
      </md>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <proof>
    <p>
      First observe that the second statement regarding invertibility follows directly from <xref ref="eq_adj_form"/>, since in this case setting <m>B=\frac{1}{\det A}\, \adj A</m> we have
      <md>
        AB=BA=\frac{1}{\det A}(A\adj A)=\frac{1}{\det A}((\det A)\, I)=I
      </md>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Thus it suffices to prove <xref ref="eq_adj_form"/>. To do so, we must show that
      <md>
      [A\, \adj A]_{i,j}=[\adj A\, A]_{i,j}=
        \begin{cases} \det A\amp \text{if } i=j\\
                  0 \amp \text{if } i\ne j
          \end{cases}
      </md>.
    </p>
      <case>
       <title>Case: <m>i=j</m></title>
      <p>
      In this case we have
      <md>
        <mrow> [A\, \adj A]_{i,j} \amp =\sum_{k=1}^na_{ik}[\adj A]_{ki}  </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp =\sum_{k=1}^n a_{ik}((-1)^{i+k}M_{ik}) \amp (<xref ref="d_adjoint"/> )</mrow>
        <mrow>  \amp =\sum_{k=1}^n(-1)^{i+k}a_{ik}M_{ik} </mrow>
        <mrow>  \amp =\det A </mrow>
        </md>.
      A similar argument shows that <m>(\adj A\, A)_{ii}=\det A</m>, though in this case we use expansion along a column.
      </p>
      </case>
      <case>
       <title>Case: <m>i\ne j</m></title>
      <p>
      When <m>i\ne j</m> we have <md>
        <mrow> [A\, \adj A]_{i,j} \amp =\sum_{k=1}^na_{ik}[\adj A]_{i,j}  </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp =\sum_{k=1}^n a_{ik}((-1)^{k+j}M_{j,k}) \amp (<xref ref="d_adjoint"/> )</mrow>
        <mrow>  \amp =\sum_{k=1}^n(-1)^{k+j}a_{ik}M_{j,k} </mrow>
        <mrow>  \amp =\det C </mrow>
        </md>,
        where <m>C</m> is the matrix obtained by replacing the <m>j</m>-th row of <m>A</m> with a copy of its <m>i</m>-th row. Since <m>C</m> has two identical rows the alternating property of <m>\det</m> implies
        <md>
          [A\, \adj A]_{i,j}=\det C=0
        </md>,
        as desired. Once again, a similar argument using expansion along a column shows that <m>[\adj A\, A]_{ij}=0</m>.
      </p>
      </case>

  </proof>

</theorem>
<example>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Use the adjoint matrix formula to compute <m>A^{-1}</m>, where
      <md>
        A=\begin{amatrix}[rrr]1\amp 2\amp 1 \\ -1\amp 1\amp -1\\ 0\amp 2\amp 2  \end{amatrix}
      </md>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      First compute <m>\det A=</m> by expanding along the third row:
      <md>
        \det A=-2(-1+1)+2(2+1)=6
      </md>.
      Next, compute
      <md>
        \adj A=\begin{amatrix}[rrr] 4\amp -2 \amp -3\\ 2\amp 2\amp 0\\ -2\amp -2\amp 3  \end{amatrix}
      </md>.
      Then we have
      <md>
        A^{-1}=\frac{1}{6}\,\adj A=\frac{1}{6}\, \begin{amatrix}[rrr] 4\amp -2 \amp -3\\ 2\amp 2\amp 0\\ -2\amp -2\amp 3  \end{amatrix}
      </md>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>
    <remark xml:id="rm_adjoint_form">
  <p>
    Before you get too excited about the adjoint matrix formula, you should know that as <m>n</m> grows, this procedure becomes much more costly in terms of number of arithmetic operations involved than our inverse algorithm based on Gauss-Jordan elimination. You get a sense of this already from the previous <m>3\times 3</m> example. In general, the Gauss-Jordan inverse algorithm is the way to go.
  </p>
</remark>
</subsection>
